; EXERCISE 3: DIFFERENCE AND COMPLEMENT
;
; Sets: theory/sets.kurt. Here A - B is the set of the elements of A that are not in B, and
; compl(A) = U - A is the complement of A in a fixed universe U. Replace each `todo` by a proof;
; don't change the `show` lines. Check with `kurt 3-complement.kurt`.
;
; `x ∉ A` is read as `¬(x ∈ A)` (set.kurt): the same, nothing to switch.

load theory/sets
const A, B

; (a) A difference is a part of the first set.
show A - B ⊂ A
proof
    todo
qed

; (b) Nothing is in a set and in its complement.
;     Hint: to show that a set is equal to ∅, prove that it is a subset of ∅ (an element would
;     give `false`, and from `false` follows anything), and that ∅ is a subset of it.
show A ∩ compl(A) = ∅
proof
    todo
qed

; (c) The complement turns the subset relation around.
show A ⊂ B ⇒ compl(B) ⊂ compl(A)
proof
    todo
qed

; (d) De Morgan's law for the union: the complement of a union is the intersection of the
;     complements.
show compl(A ∪ B) = compl(A) ∩ compl(B)
proof
    todo
qed

; (e) De Morgan's law for the intersection.
;     Hint: for an x ∈ compl(A ∩ B), split into the cases `x ∈ A` and `¬(x ∈ A)` (one of them
;     holds: Kurt knows the excluded middle). In the first case, why is x not in B?
show compl(A ∩ B) = compl(A) ∪ compl(B)
proof
    todo
qed

; (f) The complement of the complement is the set itself -- if the set is a part of U.
;     Hint: for compl(compl(A)) ⊂ A, an element x of compl(compl(A)) is not in compl(A). Prove
;     `x ∈ A` by contradiction: if `¬(x ∈ A)`, then x would be in compl(A).
show A ⊂ U ⇒ compl(compl(A)) = A
proof
    todo
qed

; (g) Think, without Kurt: why does (f) need `A ⊂ U`? Find sets A and U for which
;     compl(compl(A)) = A is false.
